A task that deletes itself spins instead of returning.

vTaskDelete (0x72f10) ends the self-delete arm with `b .` at 0x72f74: the
inlined portYIELD_WITHIN_API raises the PendSV bit, does the dsb and the isb,
and then branches to itself. The `pop {r4, r5, r6, pc}` sits at 0x72f8a, past
the not-current-task block, and only the two tests that skip the yield reach
it -- the cbz at 0x72f58 and the bne at 0x72f5e both target it. The release
falls out of the yield into a pop at the merge point, and the six bytes of the
body that differ are those two branch targets, the pop that became the branch,
and the tail word.

The loop is in the image's source, not in its compiler. Nothing GCC does turns
a fallthrough into a branch to itself, and neither -O level, nor a hot, cold or
optimize attribute on the function, nor __builtin_expect on either test of the
tail, nor writing the tail as early returns moves the release's epilogue: all
of them leave the same six bytes. The loop is also the reading the yield asks
for, since the PendSV it raises cannot be taken until vPortExitCritical has
already lowered BASEPRI, so without it the deleted task runs its own epilogue
and returns through a TCB that is already on the termination list.

Measured: with the loop the 148 bytes of vTaskDelete agree with the image once
the relocated fields are masked (abi/body_check.py).

--- a/tasks.c
+++ b/tasks.c
@@ -1188,6 +1188,11 @@
 			{
 				configASSERT( uxSchedulerSuspended == 0 );
 				portYIELD_WITHIN_API();
+
+				/* A task that deletes itself never runs again. */
+				for( ;; )
+				{
+				}
 			}
 			else
 			{
